==== Processing bars ====
Bars that are acquired through your own methods, such as Blast Furnace and PVM, can be used at the Giants' Foundry. Giants' Foundry is an alternative to traditional bar smithing if you prefer to spend more time processing and less time collecting bars or GP for Blast Furnace. Depending on the method of acquiring bars, bar type, alloy, and moulds available, you will make roughly 10x XP/bar and, aside from [[Runite bar|Runite]], will make comparable or more GP/Bar as opposed to smithing platebodies and [[High alch|high alching]] them. However, it will take much longer to process these bars compared to making platebodies, so should primarily be done if your current methods of making money aren't very strong or if the rewards from Giants' Foundry are desirable. 

'''It is NOT worth processing bars into items to then recycle at Giants' Foundry, as you will get less XP/Bar for similar or worse XP rates overall'''. This is due to the fact that processed items do not contribute the same number of bars to the Giants' Foundry crucible that it took to create the item. For example, creating Steel Claws takes 2 steel bars, but they only contribute 1 bar towards the crucible. Even if you use the most efficient items, platebodies, the extra experience gained per bar is usually less than 20% of the experience gained by using the bars for the Giants' Foundry crucible directly. 

However, it can be worthwhile to purchase premade equipment from a vendor if you have the GP for it. Buying Mithril Claws and Steel Warhammers from a vendor allows a player to fill the crucible with a Steel/Mithril alloy and the sword created will always generate a profit over the cost of the items purchased from a store. This can be an effective training method with minimal initial investment, for those that are saving their bars for other activities.

The table below is a reference for approximate GP and XP per item. 14:14 alloys are used, however, most alloys can substitute more lower tier bars for a minimal loss in metal score. 18:10 is generally the best ratio to not lose too much metal score while using bars that are quicker to make or acquire. Where possible, it is always best to use an alloy over using a single metal. 

